claimed: intent-taxonomy-and-language — requests 1 and 2 of the consumer seven, taken together because both are about what a fit knows. (1) ONE TAXONOMY BOTH TIERS READ: the model tier takes classes from Schema[I], NoModel.fit infers them from whatever labels its rows happen to carry, and nothing connects the two -- so the tiers cannot be pointed at one taxonomy without hand alignment, and a taxonomy arriving as DATA cannot reach the model tier at all, which is exactly what distillation produces. (2) LANGUAGE AS A KEY IN THE FIT: a row is (text, embedding, class) with nowhere for the language to live, so a multilingual corpus pools every language into one boundary -- and the per-language arms I ran were unreadable partly for this reason, which the consumer had written down BEFORE I ran them. A grouping key with a pooled fallback where a language is too thin, not new mathematics. Both must keep the classifier a pure function of a message.
